  "query_preview": "..\nAs a surface high exits our region, a weak surface trough will be\nin place to advect cooler temperatures into our CWA today. A high\npressure center aloft, causing a weak rex block configuration in \nthe southwest US, will promote NW flow aloft. A ridge will then \nbuild and disrupt this NW flow aloft, causing winds to veer SW \nthroughout Saturday. Ridging conditions with SW flow aloft, will \nusher in warmer temperatures. Upper-level perturbations may \nlocally heighten forecast temperatures.\n\nThe rex block setup in the southwestern US will be short-lived as\na shortwave trough ejects back into the main flow; causing the \nflow to produce conditions that cannot maintain warm temperatures,\ndue to the absence of a ridge. On Sunday, expect a drop in \ntemperatures with a cold frontal passage Sunday morning, with \nnorthwesterly flow from Canada. High pressure filters in behind \nthe cold front, bringing warmer than average temperatures into \nMonday, onward. \n\nAn upper-level trough enters the synoptic picture come Tuesday,\ndigging from the Gulf of Alaska into the desert southwest region.\nThis trough will cause SW/SSW flow aloft, creating ridging \nconditions aloft for our CWA. Anticipate slightly warmer than \naverage temperatures to advect their way from the southwestern US\nto our region. Expect dry, mostly sunny conditions throughout the\nforecast period.\n\nFORECAST CONFIDENCE & DEVIATIONS: \n\n- There is high confidence in conditions remaining unchanging\n  throughout the forecast period.\n\n- Temperatures deviating higher in latest runs of NBM, regarding\n  next week.\n\n- After Wednesday, NWP guidance begins to prompt obvious\n  uncertainty between the GEFS and EPS, as for PoPs, heights, \n  and a wider spread of temperatures are seen. \n\nFINDLEY",
